{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1958-SWP-design-I",
  "Source": "P. Baumeister, \"Design of multilayer filters by successive approximations,\" J. Opt. Soc. Am. 48, 955-958 (1958). doi:10.1364/JOSA.48.000955 - Table I (p. 956), column 'Design I'",
  "Notes": "Design I: the starting quarter-wave stack (16 films a quarter wave at 599 nm) plus an outer eighth-wave (quarter wave at 300 nm) low-index layer, suggested by A. F. Turner. Short-wavelength pass filter: low reflectance 400-490 nm, high reflectance at longer wavelengths.\nSpecification: Low reflectance at short wavelengths (400-490 nm) and high reflectance at long wavelengths (~520-700 nm); refining aimed at reducing the reflectance peaks at 484 and 446 nm without appreciably altering the high-reflectance region.\nReported: Text p. 956: computed peak reflectance of the stack 99.90 % (design I). Text: design I has undesirable reflectance peaks at 484 and 446 nm (Fig. 2). Read from Fig. 2 (approximate, not printed numbers): R ~0.33 at ~484 nm, ~0.11 at ~446 nm, small ripples ~0.03-0.05 near 420 nm, R minima near zero between; Fig. 1 (dashed): R rises steeply from ~0.3 near 500 nm to ~1.0 by ~520 nm and stays ~1.0 to ~690 nm, falling slightly toward 700 nm.\nNotes: Table I lists the films from the substrate (layer 1 next to the n = 1.52 substrate) to layer 17 next to air; the order is reversed here so layer 1 = paper's layer 17 (outermost, next to air). The table gives for each film the wavelength (in millimicrons = nm) at which its optical thickness is a quarter wave; physical thickness here = lambda_q/(4 n), rounded to 0.0001 nm, with n = 1.38 (odd layers) and n = 2.30 (even layers), non-dispersive and non-absorbing as in the paper. Normal incidence assumed (the paper does not state an angle). Substrate index 1.52 and air 1.00 as printed; back surface of the substrate not considered (massive substrate). No total thickness printed. reference_nm 599 is the quarter-wave wavelength of the original stack. Outer layer (paper's 17) is a quarter wave at 300 nm, i.e. about an eighth wave at 599 nm.\nLayers from Table I (p. 956), column 'Design I'; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours peak R 99.90 % at 604 nm; paper peak R 99.9 % (computed in the paper).",
